#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# elementwise

#% text
# `elementwise(f)(a, b, ...)` applies `f` to the corresponding elements of its arguments: lists are paired element by element at every level, other arguments are used with each element.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `elementwise(f)` is an operator form, so `elementwise(f)(xs, ys)` and `elementwise(f, xs, ys)` do not mean the same: only the first is defined.

#% item
# The values of associations are paired the same way (the keys must agree); the result is an association.

#% item
# Lists of different lengths give `failure(shape_mismatch)`; ragged lists are paired as far as they go.

#% item
# A `threaded(v)` argument is first spread over the levels of the arrays (see `threaded`).

#% item
# `elementwise(f)()` is `f()`, and an argument that is no list gives `f` applied to the arguments.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
elementwise(max)([1, -1, 3, -4, 5], 0)
#% output
#= [1, 0, 3, 0, 5]

#% text
# Two lists, element by element:

#% cell
elementwise(f)([a, b], [1, 2])
#% output
#= [f(a, 1), f(b, 2)]

#% text
# A matrix with a vector: one element of the vector for each row:

#% cell
elementwise(f)([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]], [a, b])
#% output
#= [[f(1, a), f(2, a), f(3, a)], [f(4, b), f(5, b), f(6, b)]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Operating on the values of associations:

#% cell
elementwise(f)(assoc(a -> 1, b -> 2), 3)
#% output
#= assoc(a -> f(1, 3), b -> f(2, 3))

#% text
# A vector spread along the rows instead, with `threaded`:

#% cell
elementwise(f)([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]], threaded([a, b, c]))
#% output
#= [[f(1, a), f(2, b), f(3, c)], [f(4, a), f(5, b), f(6, c)]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `elementwise(f)` agrees with `map` on a vector and a scalar:

#% cell
elementwise(f)([a, b, c], 1) == map(t => f(t, 1), [a, b, c])
#% output
#= true

#% text
# With scalars only the function is applied, and an empty list gives an empty list:

#% cell
[elementwise(f)(1, 2), elementwise(f)([], []), elementwise(f)([], 1)]
#% output
#= [f(1, 2), [], []]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Lists of unequal length:

#% cell
elementwise(f)([a, b, c], [1, 2])
#% output
#! failure(shape_mismatch)

#% text
# A vector against a matrix pairs with the rows (the first index); a longer vector does not fit:

#% cell
[elementwise(f)([[1, 2], [3, 4]], [5, 6]), elementwise(f)([[1, 2], [3, 4]], [5, 6, 7])]
#% output
#= [[[f(1, 5), f(2, 5)], [f(3, 6), f(4, 6)]], failure(shape_mismatch)]

#% text
# Associations whose keys differ do not give a failure: the missing key is left in the result:

#% cell
elementwise(f)(assoc(a -> 1), assoc(b -> 2))
#% output
#! failure(shape_mismatch)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `threaded`, `map`, `thread`
